      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/321d3ca3aaf56f2e4282368f5d121eb07c1732d1f5d71a20d9bd4eaf67b8faa5.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x+2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,6)$, $B(2,-2)$, $C(-2,5)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,6)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (6) &= -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 6 &= 4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (-2) &= -2(2)+2 \\\\ -2 &= -2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x+2 \\\\ (5) &= -2(-2)+2 \\\\ 5 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ do not lie on the line.",
