    "question": {
      ":question/id": "08fbf504-5ad9-5654-8802-5a02c700352d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/40f261ede469c3452a208fe228d0bf2a45f4403233de7552be1ecf6345a7afb0.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x-1$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,3)$, $B(-4,8)$, $C(-1,-2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-1 \\\\ (3) &= -2(-2)-1 \\\\ 3 &= 3 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-4,8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-1 \\\\ (8) &= -2(-4)-1 \\\\ 8 &= 7 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-1 \\\\ (-2) &= -2(-1)-1 \\\\ -2 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $B$ and $C$ do not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
