    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6eab5ad5-59d0-5266-9f0b-ee44b5ae158e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/6ce4fd9ba87700f1c136489e3f3c8c6f70d4b9dd4a4199e994016712f2d867fd.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-3x-3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,6)$, $B(-1,0)$, $C(-3,9)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,6)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-3 \\\\ (6) &= -3(-2)-3 \\\\ 6 &= 3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-3 \\\\ (0) &= -3(-1)-3 \\\\ 0 &= 0 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,9)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x-3 \\\\ (9) &= -3(-3)-3 \\\\ 9 &= 6 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ do not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ae039ae6-7323-5e40-bc48-c47d36933b84",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
