    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fdf5de74-3357-544a-9163-2ed52a25fd89",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/3c4e7b11288b62874f0fd1a1be1cb075c2e57047e948c2340c0152edacb2ea5a.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,-2)$, $B(-2,6)$, $C(-3,6)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (-2) &= -2(1) \\\\ -2 &= -2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-2,6)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (6) &= -2(-2) \\\\ 6 &= 4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x \\\\ (6) &= -2(-3) \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ does not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cb3400a7-847e-5d47-9f70-129bea3f541b",
