    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cbc27b1d-44e9-56f7-9a99-1053c570fc5a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/cc98fc3bd965993862136e5b4fd062c0cf0f02a01c9568d46ce30bb64d2fa33d.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x-2$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,-6)$, $B(0,-1)$, $C(3,-8)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,-6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (-6) &= -2(2)-2 \\\\ -6 &= -6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,-1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (-1) &= -2(0)-2 \\\\ -1 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,-8)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-2 \\\\ (-8) &= -2(3)-2 \\\\ -8 &= -8 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ does not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8afb025a-174c-5448-bd90-c78c2c1a53b9",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
