    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6745564c-79f2-512f-bd6c-49949b61885e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/6cf6862539ac7fff8b8ad784aca850bf4872cfb1578e09f38e0db71e6beff988.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x-3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-4,2)$, $B(2,-7)$, $C(-2,2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (2) &= -2(-4)-3 \\\\ 2 &= 5 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(2,-7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (-7) &= -2(2)-3 \\\\ -7 &= -7 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (2) &= -2(-2)-3 \\\\ 2 &= 1 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ do not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "230a72ec-bda6-5134-9e07-aad2c8a6f7a0",
