    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d84b96df-0253-5ec9-a3d7-377e4f427a26",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/d80ba8c1da40ec6a523c02e3c3785c4f2964f9eff71e3fe4d5b491c747ca462c.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-3x+3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,3)$, $B(4,-9)$, $C(-2,9)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+3 \\\\ (3) &= -3(1)+3 \\\\ 3 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,-9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+3 \\\\ (-9) &= -3(4)+3 \\\\ -9 &= -9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-2,9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -3x+3 \\\\ (9) &= -3(-2)+3 \\\\ 9 &= 9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $A$ does not lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "04cca98a-425d-5b8c-95f4-0048248ed20a",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
