      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186054676/assets/76360a930100129a4a5c13a11b42f6fa2d5ff5ce132dd5b7d6ae76fd078d9800.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-2x-3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,-4)$, $B(-1,-1)$, $C(-3,2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,-4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (-4) &= -2(0)-3 \\\\ -4 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,-1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (-1) &= -2(-1)-3 \\\\ -1 &= -1 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-3,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= -2x-3 \\\\ (2) &= -2(-3)-3 \\\\ 2 &= 3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ do not lie on the line.",
